//@version=6
// The "overlay = false" setting places this indicator in a separate panel below the price chart.
// This indicator measures market cycle periodicity using multiple stochastic waves harmonically combined.
indicator("Harmonic Periodicity Matrix [Pineify]", "Harmonic Periodicity Matrix", overlay = false)

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
// Settings Section
// User-configurable inputs that control the indicator's behavior and appearance.
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// Primary Cycle Length: Determines the base lookback period for detecting market cycles.
// Higher values = longer-term cycles, lower values = shorter-term cycles.
// This is the fundamental period that the stochastic calculations will reference.
cycleLen = input.int(20, 'Primary Cycle Length', minval = 2, tooltip = "The core lookback period for detecting market cycles.")

// Smoothing Factor: Controls the weighted moving average smoothing applied to reduce noise.
// Higher values produce smoother lines but may introduce more lag.
smooth   = input.int(6, 'Smoothing Factor', minval = 1, tooltip = "Smooths out the noise in the cycle wave.")

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
// Style Section
// Color customization for visual representation of bullish, bearish, and neutral states.
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// Color for bullish (positive) cycle phase - indicates upward momentum or oversold recovery.
upColor  = input.color(#089981, 'Bullish Wave')
// Color for bearish (negative) cycle phase - indicates downward momentum or overbought distribution.
dnColor  = input.color(#F23645, 'Bearish Wave')
// Color representing equilibrium/neutral state - where bullish and bearish forces are balanced.
midColor = input.color(#787b86, 'Equilibrium')

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
// Cycle Calculations Section
// Core mathematical logic for extracting and combining cycle information.
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// Gather multi-length periodicity to form a smooth harmonic wave.
// The stochastic oscillator (%K) compares closing price relative to the high-low range over a given period.
// We use three different cycle lengths to capture multiple frequency components:
// - stoch1: Primary cycle length (as configured by user)
// - stoch2: 1.5x longer cycle for capturing longer-term trends
// - stoch3: 0.5x shorter cycle for capturing shorter-term fluctuations
// Using math.max(2, ...) ensures the period never drops below 2 to prevent calculation errors.
stoch1 = ta.stoch(close, high, low, cycleLen)
stoch2 = ta.stoch(close, high, low, math.max(2, math.round(cycleLen * 1.5)))
stoch3 = ta.stoch(close, high, low, math.max(2, math.round(cycleLen / 2)))

// Average the cycles to combine multiple timeframe perspectives into a single composite wave.
// The result is then centered at 0 by subtracting 50, giving a range of -50 to +50.
// This centering makes it easier to identify overbought (>0) and oversold (<0) conditions.
avgCycle  = (stoch1 + stoch2 + stoch3) / 3
cycleWave = ta.wma(avgCycle, smooth) - 50

// Signal Line for Crossovers
// A faster EMA (2x smoothing period) of the main cycle wave provides a reference for momentum shifts.
// When cycleWave crosses above this line, it suggests strengthening momentum.
// When cycleWave crosses below this line, it suggests weakening momentum.
signalLine = ta.ema(cycleWave, smooth * 2)

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
// Aesthetics & Gradients Section
// Visual enhancements for better interpretation of cycle states.
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// Dynamic gradient based on the strength of the cycle.
// Positive values: gradient transitions from neutral (midColor) to bullish (upColor) as values increase from 0 to 40.
// Negative values: gradient transitions from neutral (midColor) to bearish (dnColor) as values decrease from 0 to -40.
// This creates a visual "heat map" showing cycle strength and direction.
gradColor = cycleWave > 0 ? color.from_gradient(cycleWave, 0, 40, midColor, upColor) : color.from_gradient(cycleWave, -40, 0, midColor, dnColor)

// Reference lines for Overbought / Oversold
// These horizontal lines mark key threshold levels where reversals become more likely.
// Overbought zone (> 25): Market may be due for a pullback.
// Oversold zone (< -25): Market may be due for a rebound.
obLevel = hline(25, "Overbought", color=color.new(dnColor, 70), linestyle=hline.style_dashed)
osLevel = hline(-25, "Oversold", color=color.new(upColor, 70), linestyle=hline.style_dashed)
hline(0, "Equilibrium", color=color.new(color.gray, 50), linestyle=hline.style_dotted)

// Fill backgrounds for extremes
// Visual shading highlights the overbought and oversold zones for quick identification.
// OB Zone: Filled when cycle exceeds overbought level (potential exhaustion).
// OS Zone: Filled when cycle drops below oversold level (potential opportunity).
fill(obLevel, hline(50, display=display.none), color.new(dnColor, 90), title="OB Zone")
fill(osLevel, hline(-50, display=display.none), color.new(upColor, 90), title="OS Zone")

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
// Plots & Signals Section
// Rendering the visual elements and generating actionable trading signals.
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// Plot the main cycle wave with gradient coloring and thicker line (linewidth=3) for visibility.
p1 = plot(cycleWave, "Harmonic Cycle", color=gradColor, linewidth=3)
// Plot the signal line as a thinner, semi-transparent reference line.
p2 = plot(signalLine, "Signal Line", color=color.new(color.gray, 40), linewidth=1)

// Fill the space between the wave and the signal line to show momentum state.
// When cycleWave is above signalLine: bullish momentum (green fill).
// When cycleWave is below signalLine: bearish momentum (red fill).
// This "ribbon" provides at-a-glance momentum confirmation.
fillAreaColor = cycleWave > signalLine ? color.new(upColor, 75) : color.new(dnColor, 75)
fill(p1, p2, fillAreaColor, title="Momentum Ribbon")

// Actionable Trading Signals
// Buy Signal: Cycle crosses above signal line AND is in oversold territory (< -15).
// This filters for momentum reversals that originate from oversold conditions for higher probability setups.
buySignal  = ta.crossover(cycleWave, signalLine) and cycleWave < -15

// Sell Signal: Cycle crosses below signal line AND is in overbought territory (> 15).
// This filters for momentum reversals that originate from overbought conditions.
sellSignal = ta.crossunder(cycleWave, signalLine) and cycleWave > 15

// Plot buy signal as a small circle below the cycle wave when triggered.
plotshape(buySignal ? cycleWave - 8 : na, "Buy", shape.circle, location.absolute, upColor, size=size.tiny)
// Plot sell signal as a small circle above the cycle wave when triggered.
plotshape(sellSignal ? cycleWave + 8 : na, "Sell", shape.circle, location.absolute, dnColor, size=size.tiny)
